                <pb facs="1157" n="1061" sameAs="frus:frus1874/pg1061"/>

                <head>No. 722.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Sail to Mr. Davis.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >United States Consulate-General</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Havana</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1873-11-08"><hi rendition="#italic">November</hi> 8, 1873</date>.
                        (Received Nov. 13.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 298.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to my No. 295, of 5th instant,
                    transmitting a copy of a communication which I considered it my duty to address
                    to the captain-general, claiming for such of our citizens as might be found on
                    board <pb facs="1158" n="1062" xml:id="pg1062"/> the Virginius the rights and
                    considerations to which they are entitled by treaty stipulations, I have now to
                    transmit his excellency’s reply, in which it is intimated that the matter is one
                    which does not.-concern me; that the representatives of the United States in
                    Madrid, and of Spain in Washington, will see that the stipulations of the treaty
                    of 1795 are respected, and that the good relations existing between the two
                    countries will facilitate the solution of any doubt which may arise in this
                    affair.</p>
                <p>His excellency does not seem to be aware that the peculiar relations of Cuba with
                    the United States make it indispensable that their consular officer at Havana,
                    in the absence of any diplomatic agent, should have the right of addressing him
                    on other subjects than those of a purely commercial nature.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c,</p>
                <closer><signed>HENRY C. HALL,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Vice-Consul-General.</hi></signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d724-a1.xml" xpointer="d724-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
